<section_file chapter="ch11" chapter_number="11" section="sect_06" section_number="6">
  <section number="11.6" title="The Ratio and Root Tests">
    <content>
      <paragraph>
        One way to determine how quickly the terms of a series are decreasing (or increasing) is to calculate the ratios of consecutive terms. For a geometric series <math display="inline">$$\sum ar^{n - 1}$$</math> we have <math display="inline">$$\left| a_{n + 1}/a_{n} \middle| = \middle| r \right|$$</math> for all <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> , and the series converges if <math display="inline">$$\left| r \middle| &lt; 1 \right.$$</math> . The Ratio Test tells us that for any series, if the ratios <math display="inline">$$\left| a_{n + 1}/a_{n} \right|$$</math> approach a number less than <math display="inline">$$1$$</math> as <math display="inline">$$\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math> , then the series converges. The proofs of both the Ratio Test and the Root Test involve comparing a series with a geometric series.
      </paragraph>
    </content>
  </section>
  <subsection title="The Ratio Test">
    <paragraph>
      The following test is very useful in determining whether a given series is absolutely convergent.
    </paragraph>
    <note title="The Ratio Test">
      <list ordered="false">
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left. \middle| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = L &lt; 1$$</math> , then the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}a_{n}$$</math> is absolutely convergent (and therefore convergent).
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left. \middle| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = L &gt; 1$$</math> or <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left. \middle| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = \infty$$</math> , then the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}a_{n}$$</math> is divergent.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left. \middle| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = 1$$</math> , the Ratio Test is inconclusive; that is, no conclusion can be drawn about the convergence or divergence of <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> .
          </paragraph>
        </item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <note title="Proof">
      <list ordered="false">
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            The idea is to compare the given series with a convergent geometric series. Since <math display="inline">$$L &lt; 1$$</math> , we can choose a number <math display="inline">$$r$$</math> such that <math display="inline">$$L &lt; r &lt; 1$$</math> . Since
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = L$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            and
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$L &lt; r$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            the ratio <math display="inline">$$\left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right|$$</math> will eventually be less than <math display="inline">$$r$$</math> ; that is, there exists an integer <math display="inline">$$N$$</math> such that
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| &lt; r\quad\text{whenever}n \geqslant N$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            or, equivalently,
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\left| a_{n + 1} \right| &lt; \left| a_{n} \right|r\quad\text{whenever}n \geqslant N$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            Putting <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> successively equal to <math display="inline">$$N,{N + 1},{N + 2,\ldots}$$</math> in (1), we obtain
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\begin{array}{l}
  \left| a_{N + 1} \middle| &lt; \middle| a_{N} \middle| r \right. \\
  \left| a_{N + 2} \middle| &lt; \middle| a_{N + 1} \middle| r &lt; \middle| a_{N} \middle| r^{2} \right. \\
  \left| a_{N + 3} \middle| &lt; \middle| a_{N + 2} \middle| r &lt; \middle| a_{N} \middle| r^{3} \right.
  \end{array}$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            and, in general,
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\left| a_{N + k} \right| &lt; \left| a_{N} \right|r^{k}\qquad{for\ all}k \geqslant 1$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            Now the series
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\sum\limits_{k = 1}^{\infty}\left| a_{N} \middle| \right.r^{k} = \left| a_{N} \right|r + \left| a_{N} \right|r^{2} + \left| a_{N} \right|r^{3} + \cdots$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            is convergent because it is a geometric series with <math display="inline">$$0 &lt; r &lt; 1$$</math> . So the inequality (2), together with the Direct Comparison Test, shows that the series
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\sum\limits_{n = N + 1}^{\infty}\left| a_{n} \right| = {\sum\limits_{k = 1}^{\infty}\left. \middle| a_{N + k} \right| = \left| a_{N + 1} \right| + \left| a_{N + 2} \right| + \left| a_{N + 3} \middle| + \cdots \right.}$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            is also convergent. It follows that the series <math display="inline">$$\sum_{n = 1}^{\infty}\left| a_{n} \right|$$</math> is convergent. (Recall that a finite number of terms doesn’t affect convergence.) Therefore <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> is absolutely convergent.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\left. \left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right|\rightarrow L &gt; 1 \right.$$</math> or <math display="inline">$$\left. \left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right|\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math> , then the ratio <math display="inline">$$\left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right|$$</math> will eventually be greater than <math display="inline">$$1$$</math> ; that is, there exists an integer <math display="inline">$$N$$</math> such that
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| &gt; 1\qquad\text{whenever}n \geqslant N$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            This means that <math display="inline">$$\left| a_{n + 1} \right| &gt; \left| a_{n} \right|$$</math> whenever <math display="inline">$$n \geqslant N$$</math> and so
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            <math display="block">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n} \neq 0$$</math>
          </paragraph>
          <paragraph>
            Therefore <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> diverges by the Test for Divergence.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <example number="1">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          Test the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}{\left( {- 1} \right)^{n}\frac{n^{3}}{3^{n}}}$$</math> for absolute convergence.
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Solution  We use the Ratio Test with <math display="inline">$$a_{n} = \left( {- 1} \right)^{n}n^{3} \slash 3^{n}:$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\begin{array}{l}
  {\left. \left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = \left| \frac{\frac{\left( {- 1} \right)^{n + 1}\left( {n + 1} \right)^{3}}{3^{n + 1}}}{\frac{{({- 1})}^{n}n^{3}}{3^{n}}} \right. \right| = \frac{{({n + 1})}^{n}}{3^{n + 1}} \cdot \frac{3^{n}}{n^{3}}} \\
  \left. \phantom{\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right|} = \frac{1}{3}\left( \frac{n + 1}{n} \right)^{3} = \frac{1}{3}\left( {1 + \frac{1}{n}} \right)^{3}\rightarrow\frac{1}{3} &lt; 1 \right.
  \end{array}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Thus, by the Ratio Test, the given series is absolutely convergent.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="2">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          Test the convergence of the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{n^{n}}{n!}$$</math> .
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Solution  Since the terms <math display="inline">$$a_{n} = n^{n} \slash n!$$</math> are positive, we don’t need the absolute value signs.
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\begin{array}{l}
  {\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} = \frac{\left( {n + 1} \right)^{n + 1}}{\left( {n + 1} \right)!} \cdot \frac{n!}{n^{n}} = \frac{\left( {n + 1} \right)\left( {n + 1} \right)^{n}}{\left( {n + 1} \right)n!} \cdot \frac{n!}{n^{n}}} \\
  \left. \phantom{\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}} = \left( \frac{n + 1}{n} \right)^{n} = \left( {1 + \frac{1}{n}} \right)^{n}\rightarrow e\qquad{as}n\rightarrow\infty \right.
  \end{array}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          (see Equation 3.6.6). Since <math display="inline">$$e &gt; 1$$</math> , the given series is divergent by the Ratio Test.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <paragraph>
      Note  Although the Ratio Test works in Example 2, an easier method is to use the Test for Divergence. Since
    </paragraph>
    <paragraph>
      <math display="block">$$a_{n} = \frac{n^{n}}{n!} = \frac{n \cdot n \cdot n \cdot \cdots \cdot n}{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot \cdots \cdot n} \geqslant n$$</math>
    </paragraph>
    <paragraph>
      it follows that <math display="inline">$$a_{n}$$</math> does not approach <math display="inline">$$0$$</math> as <math display="inline">$$\left. n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math> . Therefore the given series is divergent by the Test for Divergence.
    </paragraph>
    <example number="3">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          Part (iii) of the Ratio Test says that if <math display="inline">$$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| {a_{n + 1} \slash a_{n}} \right| = 1$$</math> , then the test gives no information. For instance, let’s apply the Ratio Test to each of the following series:
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}\qquad\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{2}}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          In the first series <math display="inline">$$a_{n} = 1/n$$</math> and
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\left. \left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = \frac{1/(n + 1)}{1/n} = \frac{n}{(n + 1)}\rightarrow 1\quad{as}n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          In the second series <math display="inline">$$a_{n} = 1/n^{2}$$</math> and
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\left. \left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = \frac{1/\left( {n + 1} \right)^{2}}{1/n^{2}} = \left( \frac{n}{n + 1} \right)^{2}\rightarrow 1\quad{as}n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          In both cases the Ratio Test fails to determine whether the series converges or diverges, so we must try another test. Here the first series is the harmonic series, which we know diverges; the second series is a <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series with <math display="inline">$$p &gt; 1$$</math> , so it converges.
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          The Ratio Test is usually conclusive if the <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> th term of the series contains an exponential or a factorial, as in Examples 1 and 2. The test will always fail for <math display="inline">$$p$$</math>-series, as in Example 3.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="The Root Test">
    <paragraph>
      The following test is convenient to apply when <math display="inline">$$n$$</math> th powers occur. Its proof is similar to the proof of the Ratio Test and is left as Exercise 45.
    </paragraph>
    <note title="The Root Test">
      <list ordered="false">
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|} = L &lt; 1$$</math> , then the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}a_{n}$$</math> is absolutely convergent (and therefore convergent).
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|} = L &gt; 1$$</math> or <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|} = \infty$$</math> , then the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}a_{n}$$</math> is divergent.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
        <item>
          <paragraph>
            If <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|} = 1$$</math> , the Root Test is inconclusive.
          </paragraph>
        </item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <paragraph>
      If <math display="inline">$$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|^{}} = 1$$</math> , then part (iii) of the Root Test says that the test gives no information. The series <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> could converge or diverge. (If <math display="inline">$$L = 1$$</math> in the Ratio Test, don’t try the Root Test because <math display="inline">$$L$$</math> will again be <math display="inline">$$1$$</math> . And if <math display="inline">$$L = 1$$</math> in the Root Test, don’t try the Ratio Test because it will fail too.)
    </paragraph>
    <example number="4">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          Test the convergence of the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\left( \frac{2n + 3}{3n + 2} \right)^{n}$$</math> .
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Solution
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\begin{array}{rll}
  a_{n} &amp; = &amp; \left( \frac{2n + 3}{3n + 2} \right)^{n} \\
  \sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|^{}} &amp; = &amp; \left. \frac{2n + 3}{3n + 2} = \frac{2 + \frac{3}{n}}{3 + \frac{2}{n}}\rightarrow\frac{2}{3} &lt; 1 \right.
  \end{array}$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Thus the given series is absolutely convergent (and therefore convergent) by the Root Test.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <example number="5">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>
          Determine whether the series <math display="inline">$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\left( \frac{n + 1}{n} \right)^{n}$$</math> converges or diverges.
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Solution  Here it seems natural to apply the Root Test:
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\left. \sqrt[n]{\left| a_{n} \right|^{}} = \frac{n + 1}{n}\rightarrow 1\quad{as}n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since this limit is <math display="inline">$$1$$</math> , the Root Test is inconclusive. However, using Equation 3.6.6 we see that
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          <math display="block">$$\left. a_{n} = \left( \frac{n}{n + 1} \right)^{n} = \frac{1}{\left( \frac{n + 1}{n} \right)^{n}}\rightarrow\frac{1}{e}\quad{as}n\rightarrow\infty \right.$$</math>
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph>
          Since this limit is different from zero, the series diverges by the Test for Divergence.
        </paragraph>
      </problem>
    </example>
    <paragraph>
      Example 5 serves as a reminder that when testing a series for convergence or divergence it is often helpful to apply the Test for Divergence before attempting other tests.
    </paragraph>
  </subsection>
  <subsection number="11.6" title="Exercises">
    <exercises>
      <exercise_group>
        <problem number="1">
          <statement>
            <paragraph>
              What can you say about the series <math display="inline">$$\sum a_{n}$$</math> in each of the following cases?
            </paragraph>
            <list ordered="true">
              <item>
                <paragraph>
                  <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = 8$$</math>
                </paragraph>
                <paragraph>
                  D
                </paragraph>
              </item>
              <item>
                <paragraph>
                  <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = 0.8$$</math>
                </paragraph>
                <paragraph>
                  C
                </paragraph>
              </item>
              <item>
                <paragraph>
                  <math display="inline">$$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left| \frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \right| = 1$$</math>
                </paragraph>
                <paragraph>
                  May converge or diverge
                </paragraph>
              </item>
            </list>
          </statement>
        </problem>
        <problem number="2">
